  "concepts": [
    {
      "id": "strange-attractor",
      "name": "Attractor-generating pattern",
      "render": "lorenz",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 68,
        "locator": "p. 67",
        "match": "resemble the archetypes in their self-organized self-similarity across scale"
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw07",
        "page": 64,
        "locator": "¶109 (pdf p64)",
        "match": "a kind of readiness to produce over and over again the same or similar mythical ideas"
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "mandala",
        "serpent",
        "tree",
        "spiral",
        "uroboros",
        "circle"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alternating-current",
      "name": "Energy as an oscillation between poles",
      "render": "lorenz",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 29,
        "locator": "p. 28",
        "match": "Archetypes play a key role in the synchronic dynamics of individuation by mobilizing psychic energy into a kind of alternating current between consciousness and the unconscious."
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw06",
        "page": 194,
        "locator": "¶337 (pdf p194)",
        "match": "a current of energy necessarily presupposes two different states, or poles, without which there can be no current"
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "sun",
        "fire",
        "serpent",
        "water",
        "sun-hero",
        "helios"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cascade-into-chaos",
      "name": "Oscillation cascading into chaos",
      "render": "lorenz",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 113,
        "locator": "p. 112",
        "match": "The oscillatory nature of the tension of opposites leads to chaos when the oscillations themselves begin to oscillate"
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw14",
        "page": 272,
        "locator": "¶506 (pdf p272)",
        "match": "what looked like death and destruction will settle down into a latent state of concord"
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "dismemberment",
        "prima-materia",
        "nigredo",
        "nekyia",
        "night-sea-journey",
        "dragon"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "symmetry-building",
      "name": "Symmetry-building bifurcation",
      "render": "julia",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 92,
        "locator": "p. 91",
        "match": "Thus, they make it possible for “either-or” situations to become more symmetrical"
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw08",
        "page": 102,
        "locator": "¶189 (pdf p102)",
        "match": "The confrontation of the two positions generates a tension charged with energy and creates a living, third thing"
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "coniunctio",
        "divine-pair",
        "yin-yang",
        "tao",
        "quaternity",
        "cross"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "symbol-emergence",
      "name": "Symbols: chaos first, then symmetry",
      "render": "mandelbrot",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 115,
        "locator": "p. 114",
        "match": "Thus, symbols first generate chaos, then build symmetry."
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw05",
        "page": 215,
        "locator": "¶344 (pdf p215)",
        "match": "The symbols act as transformers, their function being to convert libido from a “lower” into a “higher” form."
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "prima-materia",
        "vessel",
        "coniunctio",
        "lapis",
        "serpent",
        "fire"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "phase-transition",
      "name": "Enantiodromia as phase transition",
      "render": "lorenz",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 172,
        "locator": "p. 171",
        "match": "For order is never pure. Like the yin and the yang, it carries within it the potential to turn into its opposite. The same is true for chaos."
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw09i",
        "page": 283,
        "locator": "¶488 (pdf p283)",
        "match": "shadow contains within it the seed of an enantiodromia, of a conversion into its opposite."
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "yin-yang",
        "tao",
        "trickster-figure",
        "mercurius",
        "dragon",
        "antichrist"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "homoclinic-point",
      "name": "Homoclinic point",
      "render": "julia",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 63,
        "locator": "p. 62",
        "match": "a homoclinic point, which designates not only where a dynamic becomes unstable, but where it becomes stable as well."
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw13",
        "page": 277,
        "locator": "¶294 (pdf p277)",
        "match": "every psychic development, whether individual or collective, possesses an optimum which, when exceeded, produces an enantiodromia, that is, turns into its opposite."
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "spiral",
        "labyrinth",
        "uroboros",
        "yin-yang"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "self-organization",
      "name": "Compensation as self-organization",
      "render": "lorenz",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 94,
        "locator": "p. 93",
        "match": "Because the shadow compensates consciousness, whenever we think we know what the shadow is, its opposite automatically begins to emerge from the unconscious."
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw08",
        "page": 91,
        "locator": "pdf p91",
        "match": "Since the psyche is a self-regulating system, just as the body is"
      },
      "familyIds": [
        "dragon",
        "nigredo",
        "sol-niger",
        "antichrist",
        "desert",
        "sulphur",
        "persona"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stuck-attractor",
      "name": "A symptom as a stuck attractor",
      "render": "lorenz",
      "quote": {
        "work": "van-eenwyk",
        "page": 130,
        "locator": "p. 129",
        "match": "It simply repeats itself over and over."
      },
      "jung": {
        "work": "cw06",
        "page": 197,
        "locator": "¶346 (pdf p197)",
        "match": "A conscious capacity for one-sidedness is a sign of the highest culture, but involuntary one-sidedness, i.e., the inability to be anything but one-sided, is a sign of barbarism."
      },
